A||||5||Homicidal||Front 4X10's replacement method?||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||09-12-2000||04:05 AM||||Is it hard?  What about connecting the wires?  Any walkthroughs or tips?<P>Thanks in advance!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||09-12-2000||05:05 AM||||<UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Pop off speaker panels<BR><LI>Unscrew the 4 bolts that hold the speaker in (don't remember what size..)<BR><LI>Unplug Connector from original speaker<BR><LI>Snip wires at the connector, unless you are putting in another Delco 4x10<BR><LI>Either solder the wires directly to the new speaker leads, or, if the new speaker came with it's own speaker plugs (my pioneer's did) splice those on the speaker wire<BR><LI>Hook up the new speaker<BR><LI>Installation is the reverse of removal  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR></UL><P>As for which wire is + or -, it doesn't really matter, as long as both speakers are hooked up the same. i.e. red wires on + and black wires on -.. except I don't think they're red and black wires.. orange and white if I remember right.  Anywho it's pretty straighforward once you get started  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||09-12-2000||08:24 AM||||if you havent purchased them yet, I recommend looking at the radio shack factory replacement speakers.... they sound as good or better than the pioneer ones I bought.<P>also, dont worry about blowing them up, most cd players only send 12 wats rms (even though they are rated at 45x4, the 45x4 is at like 2000 hz for a tenth of a second), but honestly they are really nice speakers for lower end users.........but now if you want quality go with boston acoustics or clarion component sets and use plates!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||09-12-2000||09:13 AM||||Does anyone have any experience with "Legacy" speakers? I'm not a "high-end" car stereo user. I just want a nice sounding speaker for a stock system.<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||09-12-2000||11:55 AM||||legacy kind of groups in with brands like pyrimid and power acoustic. not well thought of but that could be predjudice on my part. if you have to stay with 4x10's i'd say pioneer.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||09-12-2000||07:25 PM||||Formula - FWIW my Pioneer DEH-P5200 is rated at 22 watts RMS, and 40 or 45 peak.<P>The reason I'd stay away from the Radio Shack stuff is they probably have cheap surrounds that will crack and crumble in a few years, and cheap cones that distort easily.  My Pioneers cost my $90 for the pair at Best Buy, and I've used them for over 2 years.. I don't have my speaker grills on so they've been beat on by the sun.. they've been rained on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> and they still sound like they did when they were new.  I've had good luck with Pioneer.<P>And if you want real sound stay away from 4x10's... get some 5 1/4's or similar round speaker.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
